{"Warmer it is. I added a proper hello and made it clear there's no pressure:":"c-ef705d1ebb","Here's the two-second version:":"c-2439f36bb2","Okay, a little more polished:":"c-ef123a2b61","I'd send it the moment you say so. This is only a preview, though, so nothing leaves this page. On your PC it goes out from your own Gmail, and I'll tell you once it's gone.":"c-e416b4da65","Done. On your PC I'd put those blocks straight into your calendar and give you a heads-up ten minutes before each one. Want a nudge on Friday to plan next week, too?":"c-6944eb4c9f","Here's how I'd split it. Tonight, outline the essay, thirty minutes tops. Wednesday, write the rough draft. Thursday, tidy it up and hand it in before dinner. Friday morning, twenty minutes of networking flashcards. I'll coach you through the quiz topics, but the answers are all yours.":"c-b09c754c91","Okay, your turn: 3x minus 4 equals 11. What's x? Take your time.":"c-881203b89a","Here's the short version: this page is about me. I live on your Windows PC, I can see what's on your screen, and I'll talk things through with you or just do the clicking and typing. I'm free, I always ask before anything big, and I come with you to your phone.":"c-8e71e38ad7","Let's make it doable. On your PC I'd read your real calendar first, but for a typical week I'd put deep work on Tuesday and Thursday mornings, keep Wednesday light, and leave Friday afternoon open so nothing spills into the weekend. Want me to block those times?":"c-0d1fd5cfc5","Here's a first pass. Short, friendly, and not pushy:":"c-8340192abc","Sure! Think of it like a balance. First, undo the plus three: take 3 off both sides, so 2x is 8. Then undo the times two by halving both sides, and x is 4. Quick check: two fours are 8, plus 3 makes 11. It works.":"c-8ebceda7d7","In this example you've got two: a history essay Thursday night and a networking quiz Friday morning. I'd start the essay tonight. Even a rough outline takes the pressure off. Want me to make 